ty, yet the evaluated model uses whole-discharge standardization. The only causal-normalization evidence is +0.22 to +0.30 over PCHIP under an older protocol. That is measured against PCHIP, which a CES-only running mean also beats by +0.05 to +0.17, so it says nothing about the margin over the causal GP under causal normalization. The intro use-case sentence and the conclusion (\"For an estimate that cannot wait ...\") still frame a deployable result the paper has not tested.\n\n6. Table 3 / ml-3 is still open. The \"No fast\" cut mean of -0.125 (below PCHIP) sits next to a running mean that is +0.05 to +0.17 above PCHIP. That shows evaluation-time zeroing as out-of-distribution damage, yet l.565-566 still reads it as \"history is indispensable\" evidence. The contradiction is not explained.\n\n7. Unsupported universal claim at l.621-623: \"The remaining 22-28 % is the network's nonlinear event handling, which no low-order function of the fast-channel history reproduces.\" Only a few forms were tried. The event-row evidence (l.571-573) uses only seeds 42 and 1.\n\n8. Flow and readability:\n- Results are scattered through the Model section: the A3 coupling bound, the gate result, context truncation and the surrogate's standalone skill (\"These are measurements of the model\", an odd sentence) all appear before Evaluation and Results.\n- The abstract still has about 19 quantities and 5 intervals (the ledger asked for about 9). +0.113, +0.265 and +0.221 each recur 3-4 times.\n- \"Running mean\" names two different estimators: the unfitted one in stage 1 (l.352) and the VAL-tuned 1-parameter EWMA in the Comparators paragraph, Table 1 and the abstract. Neither is defined in §5 (alpha, its grid, VAL tuning).\n- The MNAR quintile rates (0.77-4.45 %) are all below the 8.2 % overall missing rate, and the target and denominator are not stated.\n- The 72.8 % mask-agreement sentence sits in the \"Held V_rot values\" paragraph, where it does not follow. 72.8 % is also about wha